[B]The reviewers were right:[/B] the previous release animated the board and then replaced the car's velocity with a mostly vertical scripted value. The visible motion and the launch were disconnected from the collision structure.

This update replaces that mechanism instead of tuning the old launch. It also rebuilds the bouncy first physical prototype: the old 40,000 kg eight-corner cage acted like a set of spring-loaded point hammers and transferred impact poorly.

[B]What changed[/B]

[LIST]
[*]The 22 x 4.4 m plank is now a dynamic two-layer JBeam box truss with 100 contact nodes, collidable surfaces, and torsional cross-bracing.
[*]The fulcrum is intentionally asymmetric: a 13.5 m car arm and 8.5 m weight arm provide roughly a 1.5:1 real lever-speed advantage.
[*]Five collinear pin nodes form a reinforced shared hinge axis and tie the trussed deck into a fixed, cross-braced base.
[*]The overhead tower now holds a physical 9,070 kg (10 US tons / 20,000 lb) counterweight distributed across a 75-node cage with a five-node, full-width striker. A breakable mechanical latch suspends it, and a fixed vertical rail keeps the fall aligned without setting its speed.
[*]Fifteen zero-preload transport latches hold the softbody lever at its exact parked pose. They and the two weight-latch beams release together before the mechanism moves, eliminating the old spawn oscillation without applying stored launch energy.
[*]Releasing the latch lets normal BeamNG gravity drop the weight 6.0 m. Five bounded hydraulic links compress through a visible 25 cm rubber mat and feed the strike directly into five 80 kg structural plank-rib nodes.
[*]Five final-stroke hydraulic catchers arrest the moving plank. Five one-way overhead snubbers dissipate the block's upward rebound without resisting its free fall. Wide rubber-faced pads remain as overtravel fail-safes.
[*]Contact through the plank, tires, and suspension supplies the car's impulse.
[*]The visible plank and weight are node-bound flexbodies. They follow the actual JBeam bodies instead of separate TSStatic animations.
[*]The combined physical shape uses the new cache-busted ericrolph_catapult_seesaw_physics_v3.dae path, preventing either older compiled shape cache from hiding the flexbodies after an update.
[*]Reset waits until the parking zone is clear, then resets and physically re-latches the prop.
[/LIST]

[B]What the script does not do[/B]

It does not set, add, or replace the subject vehicle's velocity, and it does not teleport the vehicle. Lua is limited to the countdown, physical latch release, UI/effects, cosmetic cable and winch motion, telemetry, and safe prop reset. The lever geometry—not a hidden impulse—supplies the speed advantage.

Because the launch is now physical, different cars produce different results. Mass, wheelbase, suspension, tire grip, parking position, and brakes all matter. That variability is intentional.

[B]Measured physical results[/B]

The 9,070 kg weight should reach about 10.8 m/s after a 6.0 m fall and carries roughly 534 kJ before impact. Deterministic live tests of the packaged mod on Small Grid measured:

[LIST]
[*]1,530 kg Barstow I6: 10.18 m/s at 51.5 degrees, 8.00 m (26.3 ft) above its parked height, and 19.15 m (62.8 ft) to first ground contact
[*]1,870 kg Bastion V6: 8.37 m/s at 54.0 degrees, 7.91 m (26.0 ft) above its parked height, and 15.24 m (50.0 ft) to first ground contact
[*]10.71–10.79 m/s measured block-impact speed, below 0.96 m first rebound, and below 0.014 degrees steady parked-plank motion
[/LIST]

These are reference runs, not guaranteed results. Vehicle mass, wheelbase, suspension, tire grip, parking position, brakes, deformation, early separation, and tumbling all matter. The earlier 16–22 m/s and 25 m apex estimates require a much more elastic impact than this intentionally damped mechanism and are not advertised as release targets.

The live regression test requires the release, physical impact, real plank rotation, and contact-driven fling events; rejects any scripted subject-launch event; and scans the BeamNG log for runtime errors and physics instability.

Thanks to the reviewers who called out the disconnected physics. This is the mechanism the prop should have shipped with.
